  "summary": "Textile tradition from San Juan Chamula, Chiapas, featuring distinctive patterns and symbolic designs reflecting Tzotzil Maya heritage.",
  "description": "Characterized by bold geometric patterns, symbolic crosses, and distinctive black, red, and white color combinations, created using supplementary weft techniques on backstrap looms. These textiles serve both practical and ceremonial purposes in Tzotzil communities.",
  "culturalContext": "Based on linguistic and archaeological data, scholars believe that the common ancestors of the contemporary Tzotzil and Tzeltal peoples entered Chiapas between 100 BCE and 300 CE. According to Spanish chronicles, just before the Spanish Conquest the Tzotzil exported quetzal feathers and amber to the Aztec capital of Tenochtitlán. They also produced salt from wells near Ixtapa and traded it throughout the Chiapas highlands, and continued to do so after the Conquest.",
